{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/48663-the-grand-kami-rewrite-preliminaries-and-input","id":48663,"title":"The Grand Kami Rewrite: Preliminaries and Input","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","Scion"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-01-13T19:33:58-08:00","id":48663,"title":"The Grand Kami Rewrite: Preliminaries and Input","content":"Alright, so we all know the Scion corebook is a bit of a horrendous mess, especially in regards to Associated Powers for gods. (I mean, have you <i>looked </i>at the Teotl there?) And while I saw a lot of work done for people's personal favorites (the Greeks, the Norse, etc.) I saw a dearth in Kami discussion or rewrites or anything really. So, I took it upon myself to completely rewrite them, from the ground up, Associated Powers, Pantheon Specific Purview, Opposing Titan, the whole nine yards. And now I'm about a third through this project, and I guess it's time to put what I'm thinking out there for feedback and new ideas.<br /> I'm trying to make this as mythologically accurate as possible, so if anyone has any good sources they're just dying to share, please let me know! The majority of my research has come from Davis' <i>Myths and Legends of Japan, </i>and Ashkenazi's <i>Handbook of Japanese Mythology, </i>Along with the <i>Kojiki</i> and <i>Nihongi</i> as well, though the translations online are more than a bit dated.<br /> <br /> At the moment, I've got my God Roster done, and when I go through them I'll explain my changes from the Core Books. Besides that, I have two very vauge ideas of what their opposing Titan will be, Death and Storms, which we'll talk about also later, and the basic concept for a new PSP, because while Tsukumo-Gami is very Japanese, it is neither remotely equal in power to the other PSPs, nor is it the best fit for the Kami as a whole.<br /> <br /> Also, I gave them new virtues, which are at the moment: Duty, Harmony, Intellect, Valor.<br /> <br /> <b>God Powers: The Core Book Gods</b><br /> Amaterasu: Epic Appearance, Epic Charisma, Epic Strength, Sun<br /> Amaterasu's rewrite was pretty easy. Epic Appearance and Sun are givens due to the myth when she hides in the cave, her presence as both the Sun and someone of great beauty is indisputable. I kept her with Epic Strength for the time she performed the war dance, and each strike put her into the ground up to her knees, which is something no other god does. Giving her Charisma is the main thing I'm iffy about, but her intimidation factor due to the war dance, along with her status as pantheon head made it a reasonable choice.<br /> <br /> Susano-o: Epic Wits, Chaos, Death, Sky<br /> Susanoo, on the other hand, is an odd duck. Essentially the god of everything the Kami do not like, he is nonetheless a important member of their pantheon and while he is often the antagonist in myths, his tale of the slaying of Orochi firmly places him in the roster as a God. Now, there's a lot different here. First of all, he doesn't have Psychopomp, because what? He's not a ferrymen of the dead, or a god of liminal places (his son, on the other hand...). He is more of a Death god. After he whines and complains about his mother, Izanami, being dead, Izanagi gets fed up with his shenanigans and forces him to give up the sea-plain, and banishes him to Yomi, land of the dead. I took this as a sign that he does not have Water, since otherwise he'd be more associated with it. Later, when his son Okuninushi arrives at his home, which is in Yomi, he has centipedes in his hair which are a sure sign in Japanese symbology of corruption and death. His assocation with the &quot;Weapons of Life&quot; that are later stolen from him further places him as a figure of Death and the dead. Sky is a given for the god of Storms, and Chaos was a toss up. But as a god concerned with screwing things up and making life less rigid, like when he chucks the horse through Amaterasu's wall, I figured Chaos wasn't a bad idea. Wits is iffy, but I gave it to him for his quick thinking to drug Orochi into being killable, though something else might fit better, but I haven't found it.<br /> <br /> Tsuki-Yomi: Moon?<br /> Seriously, what else can you give this guy in good faith? His one myth I can find (the slaying of Uke Mochi) doesn't lend itself to any particular purview or ability, and so I can't give him anything else without just making things up, which is counter to my intentions.<br /> <br /> Raiden: N/A<br /> Raiden is not a god. While powerful, he's a pretty antagonistic figure, especially for the Kami who don't like storms or anything else uncontrollable. He also eats children's stomachs, which tends to push him to Titan.<br /> <br /> Izanagi: N/A<br /> I'm pushing Izanagi to Titan, because of his distance from mortal affairs, his creation of the world (which puts may figures into the Titans, regardless of antagonism), and the fact that his wife is also probably a Titan.<br /> <br /> Izanami: N/A or Epic Appearance, Death, Earth, Health<br /> Izanami sits in a weird position for Scion. She pretty much hates everything because her husband defied her and left her in Yomi, which she now rules. This would at a glance make her prime Major Titan material, but she also fulfills a vital role in their cosmology as the ruler of the their Underworld. I personally lean more towards her being a Titan, with Yomi being the greater Titanrealm of Decay or Death, but I'm including her as a god in case others want the Kami to have a legitimate underworld, which is understandable. I debated giving her Stamina, but being burned alive and never coming back to life might not be the best case. Death and Epic Appearance are obvious powers, being unfathomably horrifying and the Death Goddess. Health is for her sheer amount of creation of deities, and Earth is due to the fact that she literally gave birth to islands. Though, I'm also iffy on Earth.<br /> <br /> Hachiman: Epic Intelligence, Fertility, War<br /> Hachiman is a really important god, but his stories are muddied by the fact that he may be a conglomeration of several, previous figures. War is the obvious purview for him as the War god and patron of Samurai. Epic Intelligence was also something I though was a no-brainier in his role as he is supposedly a giver of counsel and Oracle, though there's no record I can find of him giving prophecies. Fertility was a bit of a last-ditch effort. He is worshiped as a god of agriculture, but I couldn't find any myths where he specifically grew plants or taught people agriculture, but his sheer shrine worship (second only to Inari, who we'll get to later) convinced me to give him it. I debated Guardian and Stamina, but couldn't justify them.<br /> <br /> Ryujin: Epic Appearance, Epic Strength, Sky, Water<br /> Ryujin is the guy to go to when you want a Water Kami. He lives under the water, controls the tides, and is a giant dragon. Japanese dragons are huge shapeshifters, so I gave him Appearance to represent that. He gets Sky for the same reason Tlaloc does; he controls the weather. He is worshiped in festivals in both of his roles as God of the Sea and as a God of Rain, which only further cements the two purviews. I gave him Strength because he beats the proto-jellyfish that he reduces its bones to jelly and none of its descendants have bones afterwards, which is pretty hardcore Strength.<br /> <br /> Marishiten: N/A<br /> Marishiten is originally a Chinese goddess called Marici, and she's better known there than in Japan, so when I eventually (emphasis on &quot;eventually&quot;) get around to re-writing the Shen, she'll be there.<br /> <br /> <b>The New Arrivals</b><br /> Ame-no-Uzume: Epic Appearance, Epic Charisma, Artistry*<br /> Now, I know Artistry isn't a vanilla purview, so ignore it if you're not using it, but I know many games use an Industry, or Forge, or Artistry or Creation purview of some sort and as the creator of an entire art form, I figured if anyone had it, Uzume would. Now, Appearance is easy, as she's described as the &quot;Heavenly Alarming Female&quot; and uses her beauty to win over Sarutahiko in a manner very similar to Hathor with Ra over in Egypt. Charisma is a toss up, because as &quot;The Heavenly Persuader&quot;, she clearly has either Charisma or Manipulation. I gave her Charisma for her antics and stripteases bringing all the gods to uproar when they tried to convince Amaterasu to leave the cave. I'm debating Sun, but I'm not sure if she has The Glory.<br /> <br /> Inari: Epic Appearance, Animal (Fox), Artistry*, Fertility,<br /> Why Inari wasn't listed as a core god (or even a god at all) in the books is baffling, but we're rectifying that issue now. As the god with the honor of having the most shrines in Japan, Inari is seriously important. He literally does almost everything, which puts me at a loss of what to give him. As a shapeshifter who often appears in many forms, male and female, Appearance was a given. As the god of foxes and occasionally a fox themselves, I gave Inari Animal (Fox). Inari's basic role is as the god of Rice and Rice Fields, which means they definitely need Fertility. Artistry is iffy, but as a god of blacksmiths and noted weapon maker, I figure it wasn't too much of a stretch. Though there are a multitude of other powers would could give them, ranging from Charisma to Health to Magic, but none of them fit their myths the ways these do, in my opinion.<br /> <br /> Okuninushi: Epic Manipulation, Health, Magic, Psychopomp<br /> The son of Susanoo and The Lord of The Land, Okuninushi is also a big deal. As the healer of the Hare of Inaba and the god of Medicine, Health seemed to be a natural fit. He is often described as the God of Magic and a Wizard, but I'm unsure if that gives him Magic or not, since the Kami don't particularly have a concept of Fate the Western gods do. Now, I mentioned earlier that Susanoo and Okuninushi are in a myth together, which is why I gave him Manipulation. Since he's trying to win the hand of his half sister, Suseri (which Susanoo does not want), Okuninushi is given the task of cleaning out Susanoo's hair. But instead of lice, he has venomous centipedes crawling on his scalp. Suseri comes up with the plan to have Okuninushi chew nuts and red clay and spit them out to give the illusion that he was chewing the Centipedes and risking his life for his future father in law. (If I was stating Suseri, I'd give her Wits for this) Okuninushi has to pull this off, along with trying to steal all of Susanoo's Life Weapons and tie him to the rafters in his house, which I considered sneaky and manipulative enough to give him Epic Manipulation as Associated. He escaped Yomi back to the land of the living in an obvious parallel to Izanagi and Izanami, which was the foundation for giving him Psychopomp. Further in adding to him as a god between life and death, he is murdered by his brothers on many occasions, but is brought back to life each time, which again makes him between both states. Finally, when he is forced to give up the Mortal World as his domain, he gains the world of spirits and the unseen as his domain, which again, cements the liminal powers that Psychopomp deals with.<br /> <br /> Gods I debated putting on this list, but couldn't find enough evidence for them being Legend 12: Tsuki-Yomi, Sengen/Konohanasakuya-hime, Sarutahiko, Tenjin.<br /> <br /> <b>Titan Ideas</b><br /> The first is the most obvious, with it being Yomi, Titan of Death/Corruption. Being associated with negative Health boons, Death and potentially Earth, it would be headed by Izanami and other figures of death and decay. It may or may not be the same place as Tuonela, the Finnish land of the Dead. I'm not incredibly versed in Finnish myth, but I do recall on the writeup someone did that Death was also their opposing force, which would make for some interesting stories and cosmological questions.<br /> <br /> The other is Storms, which would be probably headed by Raijin and Fujin, being storm demons who like destruction and such. However, Storms is also a really great antagonist for the Polynesian Atua, which could pose some problems. This is the one I'd run if you want to play Izanami as a playable Goddess, because otherwise you run into some baffling questions like &quot;Why does she rule our Titanrealm?&quot; and the like.<br /> <br /> <b>New PSP: Harae</b><br /> Now, this only the most nebulous stage right now, which is brainstorming. Harae are the purification rituals and the like that are one of the bigger parts of the religion. However, mechanically, I'm not sure what they'd do. Give you a better resistance to Disease, Negative Health Boons, and Death Boons? I'm debating it being like the Yazata purview of Asha where it simply doesn't work id you've been recently corrupted (Death boons, Blood, that sort of thing) and haven't properly purified yourself. Now, this might put a bit of strain on characters who want to pursue Death, but that shouldn't be too much of an issue, especially since Death as a negative force is so ingrained in their cosmology as a whole to begin with. Sure, Susanoo has Death, but does anybody like him or want him around? Not really.<br /> Now, if anybody wants to brainstorm ideas, feel free to, I need all the help I can get with this.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1012,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1012-sorcerymon","user":"Sorcerymon","date":". 01-19-2014, 05:42 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1012-sorcerymon","name":"Sorcerymon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1012_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"15"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-13T20:24:29-08:00","id":48682,"title":"","content":"Regarding the Titan, I'm convinced that Izanami is a better choice than both Raijin and Fuujin. In main, this is because she is perfect as an Avatar of Death representing its spiritual contamination, which has an important role in Japanese religion that storms cannot match. Furthermore, she is someone that the kami have to acknowledge, while I suspect the strongest kami would consider Raijin and Fuujin to be beneath their notice.<br /> <br /> This is even more with the account where Izanagi and Izanami created Amaterasu, Tsukiyomi, and Susano-o as a couple, rather than the more famous tale of Izanagi managing it on his own.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1607-creticus","name":"Creticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"69"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-13T22:18:36-08:00","id":48732,"title":"","content":"I'm currently working on an RPG which delves into a lot of Shinto, so let me toss in my two cents here.<br /> <br /> <b>O-Inari</b><br /> Yes, a thousand times yes. This kami has more shrines than <u>any other god in Japan.</u> We're talking 30 thousand+ officially sanctioned shrines, and over <u>one million</u> unofficial shrines, including personal shrines, house shrines, and roadside shrines.<br /> <br /> <b>Amaterasu Omikami</b>, because yes. Her husband / brother? No, in fact, he would probably <b>not</b> be in the core book. Why? Because he is considered almost a non-person in the myths after his screw up with Amaterasu Omikami.<br /> <br /> <b>Izanagi no Mikoto </b>and <b>Izanami no Mikoto</b>. No, they shouldn't be in the core rulebook. Why? Because once Amaterasu Omikami took over Heaven, they fell into obscurity. They could be around <i>in theory</i>, but unlikely. Part of this is due to the Japanese Underworld - if you go there, you are considered <b>unclean</b>, and the only way you come back is as a yurei. Not really a thing you want to introduce to your PCs. In fact, the entire <b>point</b> of Shinto's rituals of the dead is to <b>prevent you from going to the underworld</b>. Instead, you become promoted to godhood. Every single person who's enshrined is a god. Not even a 'little god' - if you have any warrior's blood in you, and someone ticks you off, you can create storms, earthquakes, and tsunamis to show just how pissed you are. So, yeah - these two shouldn't be on the list.<br /> <br /> <b>Ame-no-Uzume-no-Mikoto</b>. Yes, she should be on the list. She's the very first shrine maiden. She is the goddess who brought Amaterasu Omikami back out into the world. She is also the one the celestial kami sent to the leader of the Kunitsukami - Sarutahiko Okami - as a wife. Sarutahiko Okami is the <b>only</b> terrestrial god to have 'Okami' in his title, and only four celestial gods have that title in their name. In fact, Sarutahiko Okami and Ame-no-Uzume-no-Mikoto are the only gods to have <u>an actual shrine in North America</u> (just north of Seattle). (This shrine also doubles as a Shrine to O-Inari, as both Ame-no-Uzume-no-Mikoto oand Sarutahiko Okami are both &quot;aspects&quot; of O-Inari).<br /> <br /> <b>The Seven Fortunes of Luck.</b> These are also Hindu and Chinese gods, but they should be available in Japan as well, because they are a big part of Japanese culture (and... two of them are also aspects of O-Inari... go figure).<br /> <br /> <b>Susano-O.</b> Yes, he's a thorn in Amaterasu's side, but he is also a <u>big damn hero</u>, and would very much play a part in immortal affairs in Japan. He redeemed himself in his sister's eyes, which is also a good thing.<br /> <br /> Really, that's about it.<br /> Amaterasu Omikami, Susano-O at the top of the chain. The Empress and her errant brother.<br /> Ame-no-Uzume-no-Mikoto, the shrine maiden, and her husband, King of the Terrestrial Gods, Sarutahiko Okami<br /> O-Inari, the god/goddess who is damn near everywhere, appearing as a dozen different gods (and has been associated with Izanagi and Izanamo no Mikoto, as well as Amaterasu's sister, and her grandson).<br /> The Seven Fortunes of Luck - which between them cover the gamut of abilities.<br /> <br /> That's 12 - enough for a pantheon, I think.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1976-tashiro","name":"Tashiro","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1976_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"40"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-13T22:25:17-08:00","id":48739,"title":"","content":"I will also put my vote in or Izanami as primary opposition, but I would suggest a Titanrealm of Decay/Corruption/Impurity as opposed to Death, with Izanami as the corruption arising from death. Now I admit, part of that request is selfish, since I'm already using Death as the opposition for the Mayan Pantheon, but I <i>do </i>think that it fits better anyway. From what I understand, the Kami don't hate death for itself, they hate death for being a vector of impurity, just one form, albeit an important one. It also fits in better with the whole ritual purification of Harae. If you are looking for other Avatars I suggest Nirriti, Lakshmi's couterpart in Hindu myth, who represents the corruption of luck and the decay of disease, and Zarich, the archdeev of Persian mythology who causes ageing and has a side business polluting plants which are the purview of her rival Ameretat. I'm a bit iffy on this, but I think the Greek Achlys, as a mistress of poisons, and of grieving and despair, would also be a good fit.<br /> <br /> As for Tsuki-Yomi, is he just a Moon God, or a God of the night sky in general? In which case Stars and Darkness might also fit him. Though I'm admit I'm mostly clutching at straws for this one.<br /> <br /> I thoroughly approve of Harmony as a core Kami Virtue.<br /> <br /> All the most obvious uses for Harae you have already stated. Have you considered the concept of Titanic Templates as a symbol of Titanic corruption, in which case the PSP might combat these in some way?<br /> <br /> And last, but not the least, thank you for doing this. The Kami are in a very sorry state at the moment, and I would love to see where this re-write of yours takes them <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> You already have me excited at the prospect of your eventual Shen re-write.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1344-samudra","name":"Samudra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1344_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"497"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-14T08:45:01-08:00","id":48994,"title":"","content":"I like Izanami as a member of the Pantheon :c","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"right\"><i>!</i></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/245-egg","name":"Egg","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar245_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"110"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-14T09:43:06-08:00","id":49057,"title":"","content":"Raiden has always been a deity to me. A scary one, but still...<br /> <br /> *shrugs* <a href=\"http://www.pantheon.org/areas/mythology/asia/japanese/articles.html\" target=\"_blank\">Plenty of Kami</a> to go around.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-14T17:53:12-08:00","id":49387,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tashiro</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/48663-the-grand-kami-rewrite-preliminaries-and-input?p=48732#post48732\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>The Seven Fortunes of Luck.</b> These are also <b>Hindu and Chinese gods,</b> but they should be available in Japan as well, because they are a big part of Japanese culture (and... two of them are also aspects of O-Inari... go figure).</div> </div> </div> </div>Bolding for emphasis. They're all pretty much Shen or Deva, which would immediately not make them Kami. Mithra, despite being worshiped in Rome and India, is on the Yazata roster. The default Scion framework doesn't allow for Gods to have multiple PSPs (that would be <i>ridiculously broken</i>) which means that they should be with their respective pantheons. For example, Benzaiten is Sarswati, and therefore is a Deva and not a Kami.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Samudra</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/48663-the-grand-kami-rewrite-preliminaries-and-input?p=48739#post48739\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As for Tsuki-Yomi, is he just a Moon God, or a God of the night sky in general? In which case Stars and Darkness might also fit him. Though I'm admit I'm mostly clutching at straws for this one.<br /> <br /> I thoroughly approve of Harmony as a core Kami Virtue.<br /> <br /> All the most obvious uses for Harae you have already stated. Have you considered the concept of Titanic Templates as a symbol of Titanic corruption, in which case the PSP might combat these in some way?<br /> <br /> And last, but not the least, thank you for doing this. The Kami are in a very sorry state at the moment, and I would love to see where this re-write of yours takes them <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> You already have me excited at the prospect of your eventual Shen re-write.</div> </div> </div> </div>Tsuki-Yomi is just the moon god. He's not like the Black Tezcatlipoca being the god of night air or anything else. He doesn't do anything else in myth, and so is probably not Legend 12. If he is, he has the exact same problem Aphrodite dose; importance but no defined powers.<br /> <br /> Though, I never thought of Titanic templates in conjunction with the PSP before! Granted, the PSP should be able to more than that, but it's interesting food for thought. By being so hyper focused against their opposing Titanrealm, they developed a weapon to counter it.<br /> <br /> Also, you're totally welcome! The Shen rewrite is a <i>long </i>way off, as you can see by the state of this project.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tashiro</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/48663-the-grand-kami-rewrite-preliminaries-and-input?p=48732#post48732\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Part of this is due to the Japanese Underworld - if you go there, you are considered <b>unclean</b>, and the only way you come back is as a yurei. Not really a thing you want to introduce to your PCs. In fact, the entire <b>point</b> of Shinto's rituals of the dead is to <b>prevent you from going to the underworld</b>. Instead, you become promoted to godhood. Every single person who's enshrined is a god. Not even a 'little god' - if you have any warrior's blood in you, and someone ticks you off, you can create storms, earthquakes, and tsunamis to show just how pissed you are. So, yeah - these two shouldn't be on the list.</div> </div> </div> </div>If I wanted argument against Izanami being playable, this would be it. The myth of Tenjin (for those of you who want to check it out) and its subsequent Kabuki play <i>Sugawara Denju Tenarai Kagami </i>show what happens when people turn into gods in Japanese myth.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1012,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1012-sorcerymon","user":"Sorcerymon","date":". 01-14-2014, 11:48 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1012-sorcerymon","name":"Sorcerymon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1012_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"15"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-14T19:32:57-08:00","id":49475,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sorcerymon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/48663-the-grand-kami-rewrite-preliminaries-and-input?p=49387#post49387\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Bolding for emphasis. They're all pretty much Shen or Deva, which would immediately not make them Kami. Mithra, despite being worshiped in Rome and India, is on the Yazata roster. The default Scion framework doesn't allow for Gods to have multiple PSPs (that would be <i>ridiculously broken</i>) which means that they should be with their respective pantheons. For example, Benzaiten is Sarswati, and therefore is a Deva and not a Kami.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>The thing is, you're going to have this problem with a number of Eastern gods. They spread from India to China, to Japan, then were adopted into the faith. This really is going to be something that needs addressing, even if it is to say 'this god is an adopted member of the Pantheon'. Actually, this is something I'm curious about - what happens if a god leaves one pantheon and joins another? Is the PSP only for natives? Can an adopted god gain a PSP? Do they lose the old one? (I'm actually against that last one, makes no sense). Alternatively, if a god has 'facets' which show up in different pantheons, perhaps the PSP for the pantheon whose aspect he's manifesting is the one available to him. (Or her).<br /> <br /> This way, Isis is Isis in the Egyptian Pantheon, but Demeter in the Greek Pantheon, and has access to the appropriate abilities depending on which Pantheon she's representing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1976-tashiro","name":"Tashiro","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1976_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"40"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-14T20:18:17-08:00","id":49501,"title":"","content":"For the sake of reference: <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ainu_mythology#Religion\" target=\"_blank\">Ainu deities</a> are sadly little known. And then we have <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Koshinto\" target=\"_blank\">Koshint\u014d</a>; <i>the original animism of J\u014dmon period Japan which is a basis of modern Shinto practices. </i>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-14T20:44:45-08:00","id":49515,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tashiro</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/48663-the-grand-kami-rewrite-preliminaries-and-input?p=49475#post49475\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The thing is, you're going to have this problem with a number of Eastern gods. They spread from India to China, to Japan, then were adopted into the faith. This really is going to be something that needs addressing, even if it is to say 'this god is an adopted member of the Pantheon'. Actually, this is something I'm curious about - what happens if a god leaves one pantheon and joins another? Is the PSP only for natives? Can an adopted god gain a PSP? Do they lose the old one? (I'm actually against that last one, makes no sense). Alternatively, if a god has 'facets' which show up in different pantheons, perhaps the PSP for the pantheon whose aspect he's manifesting is the one available to him. (Or her).<br /> <br /> This way, Isis is Isis in the Egyptian Pantheon, but Demeter in the Greek Pantheon, and has access to the appropriate abilities depending on which Pantheon she's representing.</div> </div> </div> </div>To be completely honest, this doesn't belong here, since we have a thread about it already. It'll be different in 2E anyway, which is irrelevant to this thread on the whole.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadowflame</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/48663-the-grand-kami-rewrite-preliminaries-and-input?p=49501#post49501\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">For the sake of reference: <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ainu_mythology#Religion\" target=\"_blank\">Ainu deities</a> are sadly little known. And then we have <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Koshinto\" target=\"_blank\">Koshint\u014d</a>; <i>the original animism of J\u014dmon period Japan which is a basis of modern Shinto practices. </i></div> </div> </div> </div>I love the Ainu, but I'm not sure what to do with them! I didn't include them here because if we're going stat them, they're A: Probably not Kami, seeing as they're there own thing, and B, much like the continental Gauls, probably not Legend 12.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1012-sorcerymon","name":"Sorcerymon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1012_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"15"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-15T00:20:07-08:00","id":49603,"title":"","content":"Maybe the Kamuy of Shinto were absorbed in the Shinto pantheon or were always part of the pantheon just one of the previous seven generations of Kami that arose before Izanagi.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/490-omenseer","name":"omenseer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar490_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"987"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-17T19:12:41-08:00","id":52073,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>omenseer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/48663-the-grand-kami-rewrite-preliminaries-and-input?p=49603#post49603\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Maybe the Kamuy of Shinto were absorbed in the Shinto pantheon or were always part of the pantheon just one of the previous seven generations of Kami that arose before Izanagi.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> When I do the final writeup of this project (which should hopefully be soon! I'll have PSP stuff up sometime this weekend), I'll probably put in an aside about the Kamuy being minor Kami probably along with the Ryukyuans.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1012-sorcerymon","name":"Sorcerymon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1012_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"15"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-18T11:22:40-08:00","id":52583,"title":"","content":"I don't tend to go back on my word.<br /> <br /> <b>The New PSP: Harae</b><br /> The Japanese Concept of Harae is rooted in the idea that there are corrupting forces in the universe, and to come into contact with them is to become spiritually unclean. If a Scion comes into contact with Disease, Death, or Blood, she cannot use her Harae boons for a number of days equal to her amount of Harae Boons or until she ritually purifies herself, whichever comes first. In order to count as Defiled, she must either have used a Negative Health or Death boon with a roll, or failed her resist roll on corrupting influences.<br /> <br /> <i>Purifying: </i>The purifying rituals of the Kami have trickled down to mortals, and for an enterprising Scion who either is interested in Death or finds themselves afflicted with Kegare more often than they'd like can go to a Shinto Shrine and request to be purified. Also, being above mortals in a fundamental way by being a divine, an intelligent Scion could theoretically figure out a way to purify themselves with an Intelligence + Occult roll surpassing 5 Successes. However, since such rituals require stern concentration and a bit of time, they're not possible during combat.<br /> <br /> 1. Kessai<br /> Cost: None<br /> Roll: None<br /> Taking her first step onto the path of spiritual purity, the Scion now has a supernatural avoidance to the effects of those trying to defile her. She gains a number of dice equal to her number of Harae boons on any roll to resist corruption.<br /> <br /> 2. Temizuya<br /> Cost: 1+ Legend, 1 Willpower<br /> Roll: None<br /> Having grown in power, the Scion with Temizuya can purify the hands of her compatriots, granting them a number of dice equal to her number of Harae boons to resist Corrupting influences. After spending the Legend and Willpower to activate the boon, the Scion must pay 1 Legend for each additional person she wants to cleanse.<br /> <br /> 3. Shubatsu<br /> Cost: 3 Legend + 1 Willpower<br /> Roll: Stamina + Integrity<br /> Riding on the cusp of Demigodhood, the Scion with Shubatsu can now ward a room with salt in order to purify it. Anyone (including the Scion herself) who tries to use corrupting powers while within the room suffers a penalty of -Harae dice to their activation rolls. The ward stays up for a number of days equal to the Scion\u2019s successes, after which she must perform the ritual again if she wishes to continue enjoying its effects.<br /> <br /> 4. Misogi<br /> Roll: Harae+Legend<br /> Cost: 5 Legend + 1 Willpower<br /> Now more divine than mortal, the Scion with Misogi can perform a ritual purification for herself under a source of natural falling water. This boon is unaffected by the Scion\u2019s state of Defilement, and is the only one to do so. If the Scion is defiled, it will purify her once the ritual is complete. This Boon can only be used once per story, any more uses is at the Storyteller\u2019s discretion. After the ritual is complete, the now purified Scion gains a pool of Successes equal to her activation Roll that can be spent in any manner she pleases. However, if she becomes defiled once again, that pool of bonuses vanishes, regardless of how many are left.<br /> <br /> 5. Kannushi<br /> Roll: None<br /> Cost: None<br /> The Scion is now a priest of the Kami in their own right, such is their mastery of Harae. From now on, the Scion can choose to substitute their default resist roll with their mental resist roll (Default is Will+Integrity+Legend, but can be Int/Wits/App) to resist forms of corruption. Also, the Scion may choose to replace Fortitude with Integrity on any activation or resist roll, if she sees fit.<br /> <br /> 6. Nagoshi<br /> Roll: None<br /> Cost: None<br /> The strength of the Scion\u2019s Shubatsu wards increase, now able to completely wall the Undead unless they overcome her activation roll with their own Stamina + Integrity roll.<br /> <br /> 7. Oharae<br /> Roll: Charisma + Presence<br /> Cost: Varies (See below)<br /> As the Scion prepares for their ascension, their mastery of Harae grows ever stronger. By performing the proper rituals in the presence of the people they want to purify, the Scion can purify a number of people equal to her Legend. If she can overcome the boon user\u2019s initial activation roll with her own Charisma + Presence roll, she can pay the cost of the boon to remove from her charge. Boons affected by this boon include: Curse of Frailty, Intoxicate, Control Aging, Lingering Malice, and Poltergeist Beacon. The Scion can purify a number of people this way equal to her Legend. Those who are cleansed of their afflictions this way are also considered spiritually clean, if they happen to have Harae themselves.<br /> <br /> 8. Shinzen<br /> Roll: None<br /> Cost: None<br /> As the Scion divorces himself from the mortal and becomes truly divine, he mastery of the rituals of the gods increases. From now on, his dice for Kessai become successes, but only for himself, (Temizuya is unaffected). Also, his sheer divinity increases the powers of all of his purification rituals. He may now add his number of Harae boons to any roll as dice to activate Harae boons.<br /> <br /> 9. Mitama<br /> Roll: None<br /> Cost: None or 10 Legend<br /> The Scion is now an established god at this point, and her very soul has changed to reflect this. They now possess a mitama, a honorable spirit, which harnesses their purity to smite the corrupted. Any undead or otherwise Defiled creature who attempts to strike her automatically takes a penalty in dice equal to the God\u2019s level of Harae. If she wishes to pay the legend cost, she may make that penalty become Successes for the duration of one attack. Regardless of whether or not the attack connects, the assailant has been weakened by the Scion\u2019s sheer purity, causing the next attack to hit them to deal Aggravated damage.<br /> <br /> 10. Kagura<br /> Roll: None<br /> Cost: 30 Legend<br /> Having mastered the entire Harae purview, the Scion is rewarded with the ability to perform the divine dance, the Kagura. After spending the legend, the Scion becomes a paragon of purity, becoming immune to all negative Health boons bar The Scourge for the rest of the scene. All positive Health boons used on or by the Scion gain a bonus equal to the God\u2019s Harae Boons in Successes as long as the user is around the Kami using Kagura.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1012,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1012-sorcerymon","user":"Sorcerymon","date":". 01-19-2014, 05:51 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1012-sorcerymon","name":"Sorcerymon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1012_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"15"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-18T11:51:41-08:00","id":52614,"title":"","content":"This PSP is very in character for the Kami, I'll give you that. It just strikes me as very...narrowly focused. A PSP needs to be more generally useful. Perhaps consider expanding the definition of 'Defiled' to include anything with Dark Virtues? That would ramp up the usefulness quite a bit, and tie in to the fact that defilement isn't just physical.<br /> <br /> Also, this is more of a personal issue, but, while I understand that certain Pantheons will never like certain Purviews, isn't that something that is best represented by roleplaying? The Teotl will never stop being suspicious of a Stars using Scion, but it shouldn't cut off his access to Iztli. To effectively tell all Kami Scions that they either choose their PSP or never touch one and a half Purviews again? Especially when the Death Purview is just as helpful against the Undead as this one is, and more generally useful? Perhaps letting Misogi be usable more often, with a commensurate change in other parameters (perhaps subsequent uses do not replenish the pool of bonus dice?) might help counteract this?<br /> <br /> I really do appreciate the work you've done on this, but I don't like it as it is at the moment <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/frown.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Frown\" smilieid=\"11\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1344,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1344-samudra","user":"Samudra","date":". 01-18-2014, 03:19 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1344-samudra","name":"Samudra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1344_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"497"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-18T13:20:51-08:00","id":52712,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Samudra</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/48663-the-grand-kami-rewrite-preliminaries-and-input?p=52614#post52614\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also, this is more of a personal issue, but, while I understand that certain Pantheons will never like certain Purviews, isn't that something that is best represented by roleplaying? The Teotl will never stop being suspicious of a Stars using Scion, but it shouldn't cut off his access to Iztli. To effectively tell all Kami Scions that they either choose their PSP or never touch one and a half Purviews again? Especially when the Death Purview is just as helpful against the Undead as this one is, and more generally useful? Perhaps letting Misogi be usable more often..</div> </div> </div> </div>The problem being that you can't just have a character have Death and not be spiritually defiled. In Shinto, it just doesn't work that way. Simply being <i>around </i>a corpse is enough of a cause to be defiled. If you look at Asha, which is based in the idea of Absolute Truth, completely cuts off if you or somebody around lies and you don't catch it. It sucks, but you can't simply roleplay something so central to a the Purview's concept like that.<br /> <br /> Also, I think you missed something. Misogi the Boon is not the only way to be purified. A Scion could theoretically go to a shrine and get a ceremony done. Misogi is there to give the Scion a benefit to purifying themselves. A Scion with enough Occult should probably know how to purify themselves anyway, it's not some insurmountable obstacle. This is something you <i>can </i>roleplay around, especially if your ST doesn't call for a roll. <br /> <br /> Though, this conversation did bring up a good point I didn't put in the actual write because I never thought of it. It's supposed to be Boons you use with Rolls. Death Senses should not corrupt you, that makes no sense. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Samudra</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/48663-the-grand-kami-rewrite-preliminaries-and-input?p=52614#post52614\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Perhaps consider expanding the definition of 'Defiled' to include anything with Dark Virtues?</div> </div> </div> </div>But that makes the Kami better at fighting Titans <i>EVERYWHERE</i>, which doesn't seem too fair either, especially when the concept of Harae is so focused. Maybe extending the protection to Chaos boons or something of the sort?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1012-sorcerymon","name":"Sorcerymon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1012_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"15"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-18T13:49:54-08:00","id":52736,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sorcerymon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/48663-the-grand-kami-rewrite-preliminaries-and-input?p=52712#post52712\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also, I think you missed something. Misogi the Boon is not the only way to be purified. A Scion could theoretically go to a shrine and get a ceremony done. Misogi is there to give the Scion a benefit to purifying themselves. A Scion with enough Occult should probably know how to purify themselves anyway, it's not some insurmountable obstacle. This is something you <i>can </i>roleplay around, especially if your ST doesn't call for a roll.<br /> <br /> Though, this conversation did bring up a good point I didn't put in the actual write because I never thought of it. It's supposed to be Boons you use with Rolls. Death Senses should not corrupt you, that makes no sense.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ah, then the various procedures by which a Scion can purify himself need to be specified in the PSP description. That would help greatly. I can imagine a Scion of Susanoo using her Death powers only if absolutely necessary and then undergoing purification, all in service to Duty.<br /> <br /> Also, it might be better to go through the Boons individually than make a generalization like that. Exorcism, for eg., is a rolled Boon, and it sounds just up Harae's alley. Also, while I know the Kami attitude to corpses, what is their attitude to ghosts? Is asking an ancestor for advice considered defiling? Or am I mixing up Kami and Shen practices?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sorcerymon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/48663-the-grand-kami-rewrite-preliminaries-and-input?p=52712#post52712\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But that makes the Kami better at fighting Titans <i>EVERYWHERE</i>, which doesn't seem too fair either, especially when the concept of Harae is so focused. Maybe extending the protection to Chaos boons or something of the sort?</div> </div> </div> </div>Sigh. I reluctantly agree. I just cannot think of a way to hit the middle ground on this issue. Hmm...what are the other core tenets of Shintoism? Is there any scope, perhaps, of remaking this into a more general Shinto PSP as opposed to one that only focuses on Shinto purification rituals? I know that there is a concept of Tsumi, or taboo, but I can't think of anything there that Justice isn't doing anyway.<br /> <br /> Maybe you could scale the benefits in some way with your Virtues and the opponents Dark Virtues? Or Legend Ratings? That way the Kami are adept at mowing down the least of the Titanspawn, but only get small bonuses against top level Titan spawn and the Avatars themselves. It would be still be useful, but not to the point that the Kami steamroll all Titanic opposition and save all Pantheons everywhere.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1344-samudra","name":"Samudra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1344_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"497"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-19T15:00:05-08:00","id":53486,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Samudra</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/48663-the-grand-kami-rewrite-preliminaries-and-input?p=52736#post52736\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Ah, then the various procedures by which a Scion can purify himself need to be specified in the PSP description. That would help greatly. I can imagine a Scion of Susanoo using her Death powers only if absolutely necessary and then undergoing purification, all in service to Duty.<br /> <br /> Also, it might be better to go through the Boons individually than make a generalization like that. Exorcism, for eg., is a rolled Boon, and it sounds just up Harae's alley. Also, while I know the Kami attitude to corpses, what is their attitude to ghosts? Is asking an ancestor for advice considered defiling? Or am I mixing up Kami and Shen practices?</div> </div> </div> </div>Point 1. Agreed, put that in the Post right under the description. Also, there's a bit of a failsafe already written into the Purview. You can you use your Harae boons after a number of days equal to your number of Harae Boons. Same as Asha.<br /> Point 2. This is something I've been debating, but I'm not sure how sold I am on it. Also, I literally cannot find anything on Ghosts and Shinto that doesn't say they're not defiled. Tashiro made and excellent point earlier on why. It's probably a Shen thing.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Samudra</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/48663-the-grand-kami-rewrite-preliminaries-and-input?p=52736#post52736\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Sigh. I reluctantly agree. I just cannot think of a way to hit the middle ground on this issue. Hmm...what are the other core tenets of Shintoism? Is there any scope, perhaps, of remaking this into a more general Shinto PSP as opposed to one that only focuses on Shinto purification rituals? I know that there is a concept of Tsumi, or taboo, but I can't think of anything there that Justice isn't doing anyway.</div> </div> </div> </div>Point 3. If you do a &quot;Generic Insert Culture Here&quot; purview, you get Companion's Enech. A collection of unrelated powers and concepts that don't have the cohesion to really be a purview.<br /> Point 4. Tsumi is one of the things that Harae covers in actual Shintoism. It's essentially the same thing as Kegare, since it also covers spirtual pollution. The Law aspects of it would be a Justice thing, on that we agree.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1012,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1012-sorcerymon","user":"Sorcerymon","date":". 01-19-2014, 06:29 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1012-sorcerymon","name":"Sorcerymon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1012_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"15"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-20T17:51:44-08:00","id":54355,"title":"","content":"Alright! So, this project is complete! It's up at the link below. It's got the Gods, Their PSP, The Titans and even some Minor gods.<br /> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1q26obtxj5RVIDKc-49_rumA6BBBkCtngoXMKByxhOVs/edit#\" target=\"_blank\">https://docs.google.com/document/d/1q26obtxj5RVIDKc-49_rumA6BBBkCtngoXMKByxhOVs/</a><br /> Time to move on to the Shen!<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1012,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1012-sorcerymon","user":"Sorcerymon","date":". 01-20-2014, 08:56 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1012-sorcerymon","name":"Sorcerymon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1012_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"15"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-31T13:43:48-08:00","id":62440,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Samudra</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/48663-the-grand-kami-rewrite-preliminaries-and-input?p=52736#post52736\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hmm...what are the other core tenets of Shintoism? Is there any scope, perhaps, of remaking this into a more general Shinto PSP as opposed to one that only focuses on Shinto purification rituals? I know that there is a concept of Tsumi, or taboo, but I can't think of anything there that Justice isn't doing anyway.</div> </div> </div> </div>As a member of the faith, let me put in a few cents worth which is going to be strange.<br /> 1) Shinto has no interest in Good or Evil. There is no Cosmic Good or Cosmic Evil in the universe. The universe simply doesn't care if you're good or evil. The entire idea of morality is considered to be a human construct, which allows society to function, and is enforced by society, not by the gods. What Shinto is concerned about is <i>purity</i>, and nothing more.<br /> <br /> 2) The Kami are considered innately pure, and <i>cannot</i> perform an impure action - by their very nature. The very presence of a kami is a source of purification. Also, regarding kami and #1 - a kami is not seen as Good or Evil. A kami acts in accordance to its nature. If a kami of storms wipes out an entire village, the kami isn't declared 'evil', it is more 'well, that's what storms do', and society rebuilds from that point.<br /> <br /> 3) The definition of Kami is complex - anything that inspires, anything of nature. It doesn't matter if the subject matter is fictional or not. The forest has a kami, and has within it many kami. The shrine is the home of a kami - as is every other shrine to that kami. The wind is a kami. Your ancestors, once enshrined, became kami. The Monkey King - a person from a work of fiction, is a kami. Santa Clause is a kami. So is Harry Potter, Merlin, Abe no Seimei, Sherlock Holmes, and anyone else who has inspired people to acts of creativity.<br /> <br /> 4) The Japanese Pantheon doesn't stop at the borders of Japan. Every other god on Earth is accepted as a kami, and there's even a category for 'foreign gods'. Originally, this meant Buddhist gods, but it expanded into the Chinese and Indian pantheon, then went on from there. The Christian God is a member of the Japanese pantheon (and there's even a concept now for the 'one god with infinite aspects' now), and the Shinto faith accepts gods from any faith. Pick a god, set up a shrine, and <i>viola</i>, that god's your personal kami.<br /> <br /> Yeah, when dealing with the Shinto faith, I've found it very hard to wrestle that with Scion on a number of fronts. It is one of <i>the</i> most flexible ancient religions out there.<br /> <br /> Oh, and two other things:<br /> <br /> 5) The actions you've performed in life are completely irrelevant to who you are after death. Once you've become enshrined, you're a kami - and by default you are worthy of respect and reverence, <i>regardless of who you were</i>. The Japanese Shrine to the war dead has over a million dead, from throughout Japanese history, and is the collective shrine to millions of kami. This is perhaps one of the big problems when dealing with China and America - the shrine is seen as glorifying the dead of World War 2, who did some pretty nasty things, which is why China and the States gets upset when the Prime Minister visits the shrine. But in the Shinto mindset - it's home to countless gods. What these kami did in mortal times <i>has no relevance to who the kami are now</i>. It is in the past, thus there is no point in getting upset about it. Considering there is no 'heaven' or 'hell' in Shinto as we recognize it, it isn't like there's punishment or reward once you've passed on. And, strictly speaking, the kami of soldiers are supposed to be quite powerful - causing calamity and storms and earthquakes and floods when they're disrespected. (Which is why, the year the Japanese Prime Minister decided <i>not</i> to visit the shrine, I considered it a Big Thing. Then the tidal wave came in and hit the nuclear reactor and wiped entire villages off the map...)<br /> <br /> 6) Shinto believes firmly in spiritual purity and spiritual pollution. Negative thoughts and actions creates a 'taint' which can spread to others. Purification helps to remove this taint. There's a strong belief that the past should remain the past - sure, you must learn from history, but anything negative from the past should be left behind, because it only creates more negativity in the present. Or... obsessing about the past consumes your present and future. So, if you meet someone you've had problems with before - sure, be wary, but be polite. Don't bring up anything before unless you plan on <i>addressing it now</i>. If you aren't, don't wast brainspace being upset about what's come before, and deal with the person <i>as they are now</i>. If you wind up being negative or angry, you are going to make others negative or angry (ie, that spiritual pollution spreads).<br /> <br /> An excellent example of Shinto in action is <i>definitely</i> available by watching Miyazaki films. If you look at things like Mononoke Hime, or Spirited Away, or Ponyo, you get a firm grasp of Shinto in action. You'll notice a strong lack of evil in these stories. Sure, there's some greedy people, but nobody's actually <i>evil</i>. You'll see respect paid to the spirits, and you'll see violent creatures, but everything is acting in accordance to their nature, and you can sort of sympathize with everyone. Miyazake is like the ambassador for the Shinto Faith. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1976-tashiro","name":"Tashiro","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1976_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"40"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}